SUMMARY:In Front of the Work: An Archeology of Knowledge
DESCRIPTION:Rhonda and Anthony Moravec Gallery\, Center for Prints\, Draw
 ings\, and Photographs\, Third Floor  This hour-long gallery conversation
  brings together perspectives from contemporary artandthelaw. Guestsrefle
 ctonJones'spowerfulworkandexplorequestionsofhistory\,systemsofknowledge\,
 power\,andrepresentation.  Featured guests:I. India Thusi&#160\;is a Prof
 essor of Law and Charles L. Whistler Faculty Fellow at the Indiana Univer
 sity Maurer School of Law and a Senior Scientist at the Kinsey Institute.
  Her scholarship examines how law constructs\, regulates\, and polices ra
 ce\, sexuality\, vice\, and gender. She is the author of Policing Bodies:
  Law\, Sex Work\, and Desire in Johannesburg (Stanford University Press)\
 , an ethnographic and legal study of sex work policing in South Africa. H
 er articles have appeared in the Harvard Law Review\, New York University
  Law Review\, California Law Review\, Northwestern University Law Review\
 , Georgetown Law Journal\, UCLA Law Review\, and other leading journals.&
 #160\;Elliot Josephine Leila Reichert is an American curator\, critic\, a
 nd editor. She is the inaugural Curator of Contemporary Art at the Eskena
 zi Museum of Art at Indiana University and was a 2023-24 Propel Fellow wi
 th the Association of Art Museum Curators Foundation. Reichert has curate
 d critically acclaimed exhibitions and published widely in leading art jo
 urnals.
